{
  "dom_id": "step-q343836",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The temperature of a metal rod, measured in degrees Celsius, is given by the function $T(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the heating began. The values of $T(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $18$ | $21$ | $27$ | $30$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the temperature when $t=12$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=12$ is\n{{field-1}} degrees Celsius per hour.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{T}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{T(t)-T(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=12.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(12) & ≈\\frac{T(12)-T(12-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(12)-T(9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-27}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=12$ is $1$ degrees Celsius per hour.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
      "key": "field-1",
      "type": "blank",
      "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
